<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clara Zetkin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Clara Eißner</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clara Zetkin was a German Marxist theorist, communist activist and advocate for women's rights, born in Wiederau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. After the ban placed on socialist activity by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> in 1878, she went into exile in Zürich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and from 1891 to 1917 she edited the Social Democratic Party's women's newspaper Die Gleichheit. With Käte Duncker and others she proposed an annual women's day in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1910, and a conference she chaired in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1921 set International Women's Day on 8 March. She joined the Spartacist League, later the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and represented it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> from 1920 to 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clara-zetkin.jpg" alt="Clara Zetkin" width="330" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C_Zetkin_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Germany <b>Clara Zetkin</b> built the social-democratic women’s movement; in 1899 Qasim Amin, called the “father” of Arab feminism, wrote The Liberation of Women; in 1923 Hoda Shaarawi founded the Egyptian Feminist Union; and the Iranian Constitutional Revolution of 1905 set off an Iranian women’s movement.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
